The reusable thing is not “the prehistoric simulator.” It is a **world-to-content compiler**.

Prehistoric wildlife is just the first domain pack.

The generic engine should understand only a few primitives:

```text
ENTITY
ENVIRONMENT
CAPABILITY
RELATION
SCENARIO
RULESET
SIMULATION
EVENT
STORY
SHOT
ASSET
```

Everything else is domain-specific data.

For prehistoric wildlife:

```text
ENTITY       = animal
ENVIRONMENT  = swamp / forest / steppe
CAPABILITY   = bite / speed / armor / ambush
RELATION     = predator-of / contemporary-with
SCENARIO     = fight / hunt / survival
```

For ancient warfare:

```text
ENTITY       = unit / general / weapon
ENVIRONMENT  = battlefield / city / terrain
CAPABILITY   = range / armor / mobility / discipline
RELATION     = allied-with / fought / equipped-with
SCENARIO     = battle / siege / ambush
```

For mythology:

```text
ENTITY       = deity / monster / hero / artifact
ENVIRONMENT  = cosmology / kingdom / sacred site
CAPABILITY   = textual powers / weapons / constraints
RELATION     = parent-of / defeated-by / associated-with
SCENARIO     = encounter / quest / contest
```

So the core infrastructure stays untouched.

## I’d make every new channel a `DomainPack`

Something like:

```text
/domains
  /prehistoric
  /ancient-warfare
  /mythic-india
  /deep-ocean
  /space
```

Each pack implements:

```text
schema.ts
sources.ts
ontology.ts
scoring.ts
simulation.ts
scenario_templates.ts
story_templates.ts
visual_rules.ts
validators.ts
```

The shared engine provides everything else:

```text
/core
  ingest/
  provenance/
  graph/
  assets/
  matchup/
  simulation/
  event-log/
  story/
  shots/
  render/
  qa/
  publishing/
```

That is how you avoid rebuilding the machine.

